{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And Abraham took again a wife, and her name was Keturah.</b> He went back and married Hagar, and called her Keturah, because her deeds were pleasing like the incense offering, and likewise the expression ״וַיֹּסֶף״ implies that he married her a second time. Now we must give a reason why he called her Keturah now, after her deeds, and moreover this contradicts what Rashi explained in parashat Vayeira (Genesis 21:14): ״וַתֵּלֶךְ וַתֵּתַע״ — that she reverted to the idols of her father's house. And what seems right to me to say regarding this is that at first he divorced her at Sarah's request, for Abraham was subordinate to Sarah in prophecy, and Sarah knew through prophecy that she would ultimately revert to the idols of her father's house; and she also perceived in Ishmael her son that he was מְצַחֵק, which includes idolatry in its meaning, as it says (Exodus 32:6) ״וַיָּקוּמוּ לְצַחֵק״. Therefore she said: ״Cast out this bondwoman and her son,״ for the deeds of both of them are alike; ״for the son of this bondwoman shall not inherit with my son, with Isaac״ — he shall not inherit from the deeds of Abraham. And the thing was grievous in Abraham's eyes because he did not know through the holy spirit the faith of idolatry that was in their hearts; therefore it was said to him: ״In all that Sarah says to you, hearken to her voice,״ and from here they learned that Abraham was subordinate to Sarah in prophecy. Immediately ״וַיְשַׁלְּחֶהָ״ — he sent her away with a bill of divorce; ״וַתֵּלֶךְ וַתֵּתַע״ — just as Sarah had said. If so, since he divorced her because of the shamefulness of idolatry, it is difficult: how did he go back and take her? Therefore he comes to resolve and say that she repented, just as Ishmael her son also repented; and in order to publicize this, Abraham called her name Keturah, after the incense offering, for he publicized to all that she had repented. And for every penitent, his willful sins become like merits, and the willful sins rise up as a pleasing fragrance just like the merits. Therefore he likened her to the incense offering, for the galbanum, whose smell is foul, rises as a pleasing fragrance like all the other spices — and this is a fitting analogy for the sins of penitents, which rise up as a pleasing fragrance, as it is written (Isaiah 40:2) ״כִּי נִרְצָה עֲוֹנָהּ״ — that after repentance even the sin itself is accepted and received; therefore she is called Keturah."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:29:53.540269375Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}